Subject: [PATCH 11225/11362] [outer-loop] Serving mechanisms confirmed; 12.5
 GiB per slot kills the naive design

The codex task exercised the four capabilities that had been established only
from --help text. Three of the four transfer to a local open-weight stack and
one genuinely does not.

KV persistence works and costs 137,016 bytes per token: 22,196,640 bytes for
162 tokens, which is 12.5 GiB per slot at our pinned n_ctx of 98304. Naive
persistence across a long ARC run is not affordable at that context size, and
any design assuming it should be rewritten before being built. That arithmetic
is why the flags were exercised rather than trusted.

Restart recovery works, and it was controlled properly rather than by matching
output alone: restored runs report cache_n 162 and prompt_n 1 against a cold
control at cache_n 0 and prompt_n 163. The agent added that second observation
itself.

Reasoning state does NOT transfer. The extraction works, but replaying it is
discarded by apply-template, and pasting the text is ordinary text reuse rather
than opaque state transfer. That is the honest negative and it is Astra's
actual mechanism.

Tools work through grammar, demonstrated with a control where the grammar
overrides a BANANA request while the ungrammared request complies.

The real model produced zero engines in two complete calls, recorded as a
negative. The feature ships off and unevaluated behind a flag.

Also records that this was NOT written to memory because MEMORY.md is at its
read limit with two entries already invisible, so an addition evicts another.
The unresolved index-size decision is now preventing memory from being written.

